\f1\fs22 Sarah\'92s device began filling with evidence. One photograph showed Elvi waving from a golf cart; another featured an Elvis in a bathrobe and one shoe standing beside a flagstick. Four men raised drinks beneath a handwritten HOLY MOMMA CHRISTMAS VIGIL AND CLOSEST-TO-THE-PIN sign. Someone sent a picture of two Elvi debating the break of a putt while a UDF checkpoint operated in the background. Another showed three guitars stacked beside an Elvis asleep under a table. Sarah forwarded that one to Hayley, who wanted to know whether the guitars were resting more comfortably than their owner.\
The Airborne children came to visit friends in the mountains, and friendship underwent the sort of rapid expansion usually associated with amnesties and very large inheritances. One kid knew somebody whose parents had a house nearby. Somebody else had access to a sixteen-bedroom mansion. Another knew the security code to a place whose owners had made the strategic error of leaving their son at home. Bedrooms filled first, followed by home theatres, billiard rooms, games rooms and pieces of furniture originally selected to demonstrate wealth rather than withstand sleeping Elvi.\
Parents accustomed to controlling access to mountains, clubs and one another\'92s social calendars watched unfamiliar young people cross their lawns carrying amplifiers. Their children opened side gates. Guitars appeared beside swimming pools, followed by Elvi in swimming pools, followed by towels provided by staff who had already decided resistance would create more work. At one house, a daughter came back from the golf club with a rhythm section because they looked cold. Three Elvi acquired the guest wing at another. A third mansion accumulated enough musicians for two bands, a jam session and a protracted argument about who had permission to move the keyboard.\
Commercial accommodation disappeared shortly afterwards. Hotels filled, then motels, then campgrounds. Spare rooms vanished into friendship networks of such complexity that hosts could identify neither their guests nor the Airborne child responsible for inviting them. An Elvis asleep on a billiard table acquired a blanket and a glass of water and remained there through breakfast, by which time everyone assumed he belonged to somebody.\
The Elvi carried the music into the village with them. Guitars came through the doors of one pub, then another, then the rest; amplifiers followed, someone found a piano, someone else tuned it, and by nightfall the younger residents had worked out which pub had the strongest band, which had the best harmonies, which had the strongest drinks, and which had somehow acquired three High Elvi and a drummer capable of holding a pint through an entire number. Groups migrated between venues. Airborne kids found more friends. Elvi borrowed equipment, played requests, signed things and disappeared into parties in houses whose owners would spend the following week reconstructing their guest lists from security footage.\
The UDF checkpoints processed all of this one car at a time. Tents, food, guitars, golf equipment, Airborne kids and Elvi kept presenting themselves to soldiers whose original brief had been considerably easier to understand.\
\'93Where are you staying?\'94\
\'93Dave\'92s.\'94\
\'93Who\'92s Dave?\'94\
The Elvis leaned out the window and shouted towards the car behind. \'93Who\'92s Dave?\'94\
Three people offered different answers.\
The soldier studied the queue stretching away behind them, handed back the documents and waved the car through.\
By the next evening, soldiers on checkpoint duty knew which Elvi were playing in which pubs, while the Elvi had learned which soldiers liked which songs. An outgoing corporal told his replacement that the Piglicious Minds lot were playing somewhere up the road. The incoming corporal checked his watch before he checked the next vehicle. Later, three UDF soldiers turned up at one of the performances still in uniform and had a thoroughly good time. A fourth found them there.\
Their sergeant arrived to retrieve them during the second set.\
He stayed for the encore.\

